			'en_slug'             => '1-caring-for-yourself-in-a-foreign-land',
			'en_title'            => '1. Caring for Yourself in a Foreign Land',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'Emotional difficulties, overwhelm and longing for family are part of starting over abroad — but simple self-care practices can protect your mental health.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'A therapist\'s guide to self-care while adapting to life abroad — and why culturally sensitive support matters for immigrants in Ireland.',
			'en_content'          => '</p>
</p>
<p><em>Text: Lili Fraga</em></p>
<p><em>Therapist and Parent Educator</em></p>
</p>
<h4>Central theme: Self-care as a tool for adapting to immigration</h4>
<p>Emotional difficulties, overwhelm, missing your family and the feeling of always being in survival mode. How can you create moments of pause, connection with yourself and simple practices to protect your mental health even with little time or resources?</p>
</p>
<h2><strong>✨ Caring for Yourself in a Foreign Land</strong></h2>
</p>
<p><strong>A look at the emotional wellbeing of those starting over outside Brazil</strong></p>
<p>Living far from your country of origin can be challenging. Beyond the demands of everyday life, the immigrant faces deep emotional layers — often marked by the feeling of not belonging. Some discover themselves to be incredibly resilient and recognise their inner strength. Others decide to return to their country. And there are those who take on the challenge of seeking a new place in the world. What we do know is that we Brazilians — with our warm blood, our easy laugh and our open affection — carry far more than other people\'s eyes can see.</p>
</p>
<p>The truth is that there is no single path to happiness and wellbeing. Feeling well far from home invites us on a journey of <strong>self-discovery</strong>, where we learn to respect our limits, to accept that we will not always be understood and that we may not manage to please everyone. Sometimes we need to create our own space in order to feel at ease within discomfort.</p>
</p>
<p>And this is not just poetry. Studies show that self-care practices (such as creating routines, maintaining social bonds or spirituality) are fundamental to the mental health of immigrants and refugees, with psychological and social strategies being the most effective in preventing anxiety, sadness and chronic low mood (Mehjabeen et al., 2023). In addition, public health researchers in Ireland highlight the importance of having access to culturally sensitive services that respect our language, our history and our pace of adaptation (<u>McMahon et al., 2021</u>).</p>
<p><strong>Looking after our wellbeing is necessary.</strong> Building relationships that allow us to be who we truly are is crucial to walking this path of starting over, where emotional bridges may look fragile but can be built with affection and courage.</p>
<p>And that is why I invite you to look at <em>one moment at a time</em>, to make your choices based on your own reality and to live at peace with yourself. You will see that happiness really can be part of this nomadic life of yours — not as a fixed destination, but as a state of presence.</p>
<p><strong>If you need help, do not hesitate to ask for it.</strong> Far from home, we can build a new home — as long as we are aware of the pillars that hold it up.</p>
</p>
<p>🗓️ <strong>Next month, we will talk about: “Mental Health and Immigration – What Nobody Tells You”.</strong> We will make room to talk about anxiety, insomnia, guilt, and what to do when it feels as though we are losing our strength. </p>
</p>
<p>See you there. With affection, Lili Fraga &#8211; Therapist and Parent Educator <u><a href="http://www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve" rel="nofollow">http://www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve</a></u></p></p>
',
