		'a-caneta-emagrece-e-o-espirito-corre-atras' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'the-pen-sheds-weight-does-the-spirit-catch-up',
			'en_title'            => 'The Pen Sheds Weight… Does the Spirit Catch Up?',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'The weight-loss pen works fast — but is your nervous system ready? A systemic therapist on what rapid weight loss does to your emotions, your habits and your sense of identity.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'A systemic therapist on what rapid weight loss does to the nervous system, emotions and identity — and why support matters.',
			'en_content'          => '<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p>
<p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p>
</p>
<p>“It works fast&#8230; but did your heart get the warning?”</p>
</p>
<p>Over the last few months, I have heard the same sentence in many sessions:</p>
<p>&#8220;Aria, I am thinking about using the pen&#8230; they say it works really fast.&#8221;</p>
<p>And yes — it works. But the question almost no one asks is: what happens to the</p>
<p>nervous system, to the emotions and to the identity when the body changes too</p>
<p>fast?</p>
</p>
<p>I am not speaking against medicine — I speak as someone who follows closely what</p>
<p>happens inside people when the body changes before the mind and the heart</p>
<p>are ready.</p>
<p>Because losing weight fast is not only losing weight. It is going through a transformation that</p>
<p>involves the body, the nervous system and the way you see yourself in the world.</p>
<p>And that deserves respect — not haste.</p>
</p>
<p>“Less hunger on the plate&#8230; more silence in your emotions?”</p>
<p>Medications such as Mounjaro (tirzepatide) were created to treat type 2 diabetes.</p>
<p>They mimic hormones that reduce appetite, increase satiety and stabilise</p>
<p>glucose.</p>
<p>In practice, you eat less — and lose weight.</p>
<p>But there is something profound here: hunger is not only from the stomach. It is also from the heart.</p>
<p>Many people eat to relieve anxiety, compensate for sadness or fill emotional</p>
<p>voids. When food decreases quickly, that emotional pathway is affected too.</p>
<p>And the nervous system feels it.</p>
</p>
<p>“Your nervous system did not get the instruction manual”</p>
<p>When the body starts eating far less, fast, it goes into adaptation.</p>
<p>During use, many people report:</p>
<p>● intense satiety</p>
<p>● reduced pleasure from food</p>
<p>● nausea and fatigue</p>
<p>● mood swings</p>
<p>● a feeling of apathy or “switching off”</p>
</p>
<p>This happens because food also regulates emotions.</p>
<p>When that regulation changes too fast, the nervous system needs to find new</p>
<p>ways of balancing — and that does not always happen smoothly.</p>
<p>Some people become anxious. Others irritable. Others feel a silent emptiness.</p>
<p>It is not weakness. It is the body trying to reorganise itself.</p>
</p>
<p>“And when the pen leaves the scene&#8230; who holds the emotions?”</p>
<p>When use is interrupted, the body tries to recover its previous balance.</p>
<p>And that can bring:</p>
<p>● increased hunger</p>
<p>● the return of emotional appetite</p>
<p>● weight regain</p>
<p>● a feeling of losing control</p>
<p>But there is something even deeper: if the weight changed fast, but the emotional patterns remained, the body can return to the old behaviour — not out of failure, but out of survival.</p>
<p>The body tries to protect the old identity.</p>
<p>And that can generate shame, guilt, fear of gaining weight again — and even emotional</p>
<p>dependence on the medication.</p>
<p>I have seen people who lost many kilos&#8230; but still felt trapped inside themselves.</p>
</p>
<p>“New body, old inner story?”</p>
<p>Losing weight fast changes the body — and changes how the world looks at you.</p>
<p>More attention. More comments. More expectations.</p>
<p>And the heart is not always ready for that.</p>
<p>Some people feel proud. Others find their own reflection strange.</p>
</p>
<p>Because the body changes fast — but identity needs time.</p>
<p>There is also something delicate: for many people, weight works as emotional</p>
<p>protection.</p>
<p>Protection against exposure. Against vulnerability. Against old memories.</p>
<p>When weight drops fast, that protection can disappear — and old emotions</p>
<p>emerge.</p>
<p>And that needs to be held with care.</p>
</p>
<p>“Not every heart is ready to lose weight fast”</p>
<p>Not every emotional story can sustain fast changes.</p>
<p>Profiles that need more caution include:</p>
<p>● people with a history of binge eating</p>
<p>● intense anxiety or emotional instability</p>
<p>● a history of trauma or abuse</p>
<p>● a highly critical relationship with their body</p>
<p>● people who lose weight because of external pressure</p>
<p>It is not about forbidding. It is about understanding whether your system is ready.</p>
</p>
<p>“When the body loses weight&#8230; the soul needs to catch up”</p>
<p>From a therapeutic and energetic point of view, losing weight fast is a change of identity.</p>
<p>The mind needs to update the image it has of itself.</p>
<p>The heart needs to deal with new emotions.</p>
<p>The soul needs to integrate a new way of existing.</p>
<p>When that does not happen, misalignment appears.</p>
<p>The body changes — but inside, the feeling remains the old one.</p>
<p>Because weight is not only fat. It is history. It is memory. It is accumulated emotion.</p>
<p>If you are considering using the pen, perhaps the most important question is not:</p>
<p>&#8220;Will this work?&#8221;</p>
<p>But rather:</p>
</p>
<p>&#8220;Do I have support to sustain who I am going to become?&#8221;</p>
<p>Going through this process with therapeutic and emotional support helps to sustain:</p>
<p>● new forms of regulation</p>
<p>● new habits</p>
<p>● the reorganisation of identity</p>
<p>● the integration of the new body image</p>
<p>Not as something mystical — but as conscious care.</p>
<p>“It is not about fear&#8230; it is about awareness”</p>
<p>If you are using it, thinking about using it, or have already used it — it is not about guilt.</p>
<p>It is about awareness.</p>
<p>Medicine has its place. But true transformation happens when body,</p>
<p>mind and heart walk together.</p>
<p>Because losing weight is not only lowering the numbers on the scales.</p>
<p>It is going through a deep inner process.</p>
<p>And when that is done with support, presence and listening — the result is not only a</p>
<p>lighter body.</p>
<p>It is a lighter life.</p>
<p>And that&#8230; yes&#8230; is real transformation.</p>
',
