		'canetas-emagrecedoras-emagrecer-ficou-mais-facil-mas-e-a-saude' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'weight-loss-pens-but-what-about-your-health',
			'en_title'            => 'Weight-Loss Pens: Losing Weight Got Easier… But What About Your Health?',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'Weight-loss pens are everywhere — in consultations and in conversations between friends. A nutritionist on who they are really indicated for, and what they do to your health.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'A nutritionist on who weight-loss pens are really indicated for, the risks of unsupervised use and why nutrition matters.',
			'en_content'          => '<p><strong>Text: Nutri Marcela</strong></p>
<p><strong>Instagram: </strong><u><strong><a href="https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/" rel="nofollow">https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/</a></strong></u></p>
</p>
<p>A reflection on the physical, nutritional and emotional impacts of using these medications</p>
</p>
<p>Today I want to talk about the so-called “weight-loss pens”, a topic that is trending everywhere… on social media, in consultations and even in conversations between friends and family.</p>
</p>
<p>The movement is so strong that, in some cases, people even feel excluded for not using them, as if this were necessary to be part of society, often without thinking about their own health.</p>
</p>
<p>This ends up reinforcing a thinness standard that is often unattainable, unrealistic and, above all, unsustainable for most people.</p>
</p>
<p>💉 What are the “weight-loss pens”?</p>
</p>
<p>In fact, that is not the most accurate name. These medications were developed for the treatment of obesity and type 2 diabetes, with a medical indication and proper follow-up.</p>
</p>
<p>They work by mimicking a hormone naturally produced by our body, called GLP-1, which acts mainly in the intestine, helping to regulate hunger, increase satiety and improve blood glucose control.</p>
</p>
<p>And did you know that some of these mechanisms can also be stimulated naturally? The quality of our food, mindful eating and even the order in which we eat our food can influence satiety and our body’s response.</p>
</p>
<p>👩‍⚕️ Who are these medications indicated for?</p>
</p>
<p>These medications, known as GLP-1 analogues, are important and bring real benefits when properly indicated.</p>
</p>
<p>According to international guidelines, they are generally indicated for people with obesity (BMI ≥ 30) or overweight (BMI ≥ 27) associated with comorbidities, such as type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance, hypertension, high cholesterol or cardiovascular disease.</p>
</p>
<p>And it is important to make this clear: I am not against the use of these medications. On the contrary, they represent an important advance in health and can bring more quality of life to people who genuinely need them.</p>
</p>
<p>But the reflection remains: does everyone who is using them have an indication?</p>
</p>
<p>⚠️ Points of attention when using them</p>
</p>
<p>Today we live with an intense pursuit of fast results, especially when it comes to aesthetics. And, in this context, it is essential to look beyond the number on the scales.</p>
</p>
<p>Mental health, for example, deserves attention. Your relationship with your body, with food and with your own image can be deeply affected.</p>
</p>
<p>Another warning point is the use of these medications in an inadequate way, without medical follow-up, obtained inappropriately or even counterfeit. This represents a real risk to health.</p>
</p>
<p>🥗 The role of nutrition in this process</p>
</p>
<p>One of the effects of these medications is a reduction in appetite, along with possible side effects such as nausea, sickness and constipation.</p>
</p>
<p>But this raises an important question: how can you guarantee food with nutritional quality if you are eating much less?</p>
</p>
<p>Without proper guidance, your intake can become insufficient in important nutrients, such as protein, vitamins and minerals.</p>
</p>
<p>And that is when consequences start to appear, such as hair loss, fatigue, nutritional deficiencies and a worse quality of life.</p>
</p>
<p>In addition, eating less can lead to the loss of lean mass, that is, muscle loss, which directly affects metabolism and health in the long term. This process can increase the risk of sarcopenia and also contribute to a more pronounced weight regain in the future.</p>
</p>
<p>🧠 Hunger, behaviour and “food noise”</p>
</p>
<p>Many people report that, with the use of the medication, the so-called “food noise”, or eating noise, decreases.</p>
</p>
<p>But it is important to remember that hunger is an important physiological signal from our body. We should not be afraid of it. The goal is not to eliminate hunger, but to learn to understand it and regulate it.</p>
</p>
<p>And more: these medications do not directly treat emotional hunger. If there is no work on eating behaviour, these patterns tend to return when use is interrupted.</p>
</p>
<p>🔄 What happens afterwards?</p>
</p>
<p>One point that needs to be considered is what happens after the weight loss.</p>
</p>
<p>The body, naturally, tends to reduce metabolism as an adaptation mechanism. In other words, keeping the weight off can become a bigger challenge.</p>
</p>
<p>In addition, when there is a significant loss of muscle mass, the risk of weight regain increases even more.</p>
</p>
<p>And this leads us to an important reflection: if we are talking about medications used for chronic conditions, are we not facing treatments that may be necessary for life?</p>
</p>
<p>💚 A final reflection</p>
</p>
<p>In the pursuit of quick solutions, we often forget that health is a process.</p>
</p>
<p>Taking care of the body involves food, movement, mental health and behaviour.</p>
</p>
<p>There is nothing wrong with wanting to lose weight. But it is important to reflect: is this being done for health or only for aesthetic pressure?</p>
</p>
<p>Being thin is not a synonym for being healthy.</p>
</p>
<p>True transformation happens when we manage to build a more balanced relationship with our body and with food.</p>
</p>
<p>As Carl Jung said:</p>
<p>“What you resist, persists. What you accept, transforms.”</p>
<p>If you have an indication for the use of these medications, talk to your doctor and seek proper follow-up.</p>
</p>
<p>Now, if the motivation is only aesthetic, </p>
<p>it is worth reflecting with more care. Not everything that seems easy at the start is sustainable in the long term.</p>
<p>If you want to understand better how to take care of your food in a balanced and sustainable way, and with more awareness, I am here to help you.</p>
</p>
<p>Online and in-person appointments in Dublin and Kilcock</p>
<p>@marcela.holisticnutrition</p>
</p>
<p>With warmth,</p>
<p>Nutri Marcela 💚</p>
',
