		'como-voce-se-sente-nessa-epoca-do-ano' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'how-do-you-feel-at-this-time-of-year',
			'en_title'            => 'How Do You Feel at This Time of Year?',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'Short days, grey skies and constant cold affect your mood and energy. Recognising seasonal affective disorder is the first step to caring for yourself.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'How autumn and winter in Ireland affect your mood and energy, and practical ways to care for your mental health through the darker months.',
			'en_content'          => '</p>
<p><em>Lili Fraga &#8211; </em></p>
<p><em>Therapist and Parent Educator </em></p>
</p>
<h4>Have you ever stopped to think about how your body and your emotions react to the arrival of autumn and winter here in Ireland?</h4>
<p>Of course there are people who enjoy the chill, the silence, being cosy in their own little corner — and that is perfectly fine if that is you. But for many of us, especially those who came from tropical countries or from places with well-defined seasons, this time of year can be challenging.</p>
<p>The short days, the grey sky and the constant cold affect our mood, our energy and even our self-esteem. This has a name: scientifically, we call it <strong>Seasonal Affective Disorder (SAD)</strong> — a type of depression related to the change of season, common in countries of the northern hemisphere (GUZMAN et al., 2007; SAHEER et al., 2013).</p>
<p>In addition, studies with immigrants show that the <strong>feeling of isolation and low exposure to natural light</strong> intensify symptoms such as sadness, fatigue, irritability and lower immunity (SAHEER et al., 2013).</p>
<p>That is why, now more than ever, this is a time to <strong>observe your own behaviour with tenderness and compassion</strong>. Recognising what affects you is the first step towards turning discomfort into care.</p>
</p>
<h3><strong>🌱 What if we met up?</strong></h3>
<p>Know that <strong>you have a real opportunity to meet other people, laugh, talk and feel part of something bigger</strong>. The <strong>group of Brazilians in Portlaoise is turning 5 years old</strong> — and, to celebrate this beautiful journey of mutual support and connection, <strong>there will be a special in-person meet-up in the park</strong>. <em><strong>(Event postponed, a new date will be announced).</strong></em></p>
<p><strong>Contact with nature and with other people is a powerful way of looking after your mental health</strong>, especially at this time of year.</p>
<p>Studies prove that <strong>frequent exposure to green spaces is directly associated with reduced anxiety and depression</strong>, as well as promoting the release of hormones related to wellbeing (TANG et al., 2023). Another survey reveals that <strong>20 to 30 minutes in natural environments</strong> are already enough to reduce cortisol levels and increase the feeling of social connection (WHO, 2021; GASCON et al., 2015).</p>
<p>Being outdoors, with people who share the same journey, is an invitation to welcome, affection and belonging.</p>
</p>
<h3><strong>💡 Practical tips for looking after yourself in winter:</strong></h3>
<p>✅ <strong>Make the most of natural light</strong>, even if only for a few minutes a day.</p>
<p>✅ <strong>Move your body</strong> — walks, dancing or stretching already help a great deal. </p>
<p>✅ <strong>Eat with intention</strong> — bananas, nuts, oats and cocoa stimulate serotonin.</p>
<p>✅ <strong>Talk to someone</strong> — join groups, call a friend, seek therapy.</p>
<p>✅ <strong>Plan small weekly pleasures</strong> — a coffee, a book, a hot bath.</p>
<p>✅ <strong>And if you can: come to the in-person meet-up!</strong> Being with other people warms the heart.</p>
</p>
<p>✨ <strong>Do not let winter freeze you from the inside.</strong> Let us keep going together, creating human warmth, looking after our mental health and celebrating every step of this journey away from home — but ever closer to ourselves.</p>
</p>
<p>📍<strong>See you at the park?</strong> It will be beautiful to share this moment with you.</p>
<p>With affection, Lili Fraga &#8211; Therapist and Parent Educator <u><a href="http://www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve" rel="nofollow">http://www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve</a></u></p></p>
',
