		),
		'construindo-rede-de-apoio-longe-de-casa' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'building-a-support-network-far-from-home',
			'en_title'            => 'Building a Support Network Far From Home',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'Far from home, expatriate mothers can rebuild the support network they miss — here are practical, accessible ways to start in Ireland.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'Practical ways for immigrant mothers in Ireland to build a support network, from parent and toddler groups to bilingual playgroups.',
			'en_content'          => '<p><em>Written by: Lili Fraga &#8211; Family &amp; Children Intercultural &amp; Emotional Development</em></p>
<p><em><strong>Connections that strengthen immigrant wellbeing</strong></em></p>
</p>
<p>Living outside your country of origin is, in itself, a dive into the unknown. But for expatriate mothers, the challenges multiply. Motherhood away from home carries layers of longing, tiredness, fear and, often, <strong>the temporary loss of your own identity</strong>. Who you were before you left seems distant — and now, as well as a mother, you are also an immigrant, starting from scratch in a place where you do not always speak the language or understand the social dynamics.</p>
<p>The absence of your support network — that grandmother, that friend, that neighbour — is sorely felt. The routine becomes heavier, rest becomes rarer, and self-esteem can begin to weaken. The good news? <strong>Even far from home, it is possible to build new networks.</strong> Perhaps different from the ones you knew, but just as powerful, genuine and welcoming.</p>
<p>Help does not always appear spontaneously, and that is okay. But know this: <strong>you can find or create spaces of support that hold you emotionally, help with the challenges of everyday life and remind you that you are not alone.</strong></p>
</p>
<h3><strong>💡 Practical strategies for finding your network in Ireland</strong></h3>
<p>Here are some real, accessible ways to step out of isolation and start connecting with other people who share similar values:</p>
<h4><strong>👩‍👧‍👦 1. Groups for mothers and fathers (Parent &amp; Toddler Groups)</strong></h4>
<p>Community spaces where mothers (or fathers) and their small children gather to play, socialise and swap experiences. Many are free or cost a token amount. 🔎 Where to find them: <em>Family Resource Centres</em>, local churches, crèches or community centres. E.g.: <strong>A Mother&#8217;s Embrace</strong> (Waterford), <strong>Barnardos</strong>, <strong>TUSLA parent support groups</strong>.</p>
<h4><strong>🤱 2. Bilingual or cultural playgroups</strong></h4>
<p>Taking part in groups that respect and celebrate your culture (such as groups of Brazilian or Latin American mothers) helps you feel seen and welcomed. 🔎 Look for groups on WhatsApp, Facebook (“Brasileiras na Irlanda”, “Mães em Dublin”, etc.), or in community schools.</p>
<h4><strong>☕ 3. Cafés for immigrants and themed in-person meet-ups</strong></h4>
<p>Moments designed to create connections: conversation circles, workshops, wellbeing and motherhood events. 🔎 Follow local projects such as Mum talks <u><a href="https://www.instagram.com/mum_talks/" rel="nofollow">https://www.instagram.com/mum_talks/</a></u> </p>
<h4><strong>🧘 4. Activities that combine self-care and company</strong></h4>
<p>Yoga classes, walking groups, art workshops or guided meditation with other mothers. A lovely way of looking after yourself while socialising. 🔎 Search Eventbrite, Meetup or Instagram for local activities using keywords such as “mothers”, “community wellness”, “creative circles”.</p>
<p>A few apps that can help you find the right group for you: &#8211; <u>meetup.com</u>&#8211; <u>peanut-app.io</u>&#8211; <u>letsmush.com</u>&#8211; <u>nextdoor.ie</u></p>
<h4><strong>📱 5. Create your own network!</strong></h4>
<p>If you have not found anything that fits you, <strong>create it!</strong> A coffee at home with other mothers, a virtual support group and a picnic in the park. Someone is always waiting for that invitation.</p>
<h3><strong>💛 Wrapping up&#8230;</strong></h3>
<p><strong>Connections heal. Belonging strengthens. Being seen transforms.</strong> Even far from your homeland, you can — little by little — rebuild your support network, surround yourself with people who welcome who you are today, and experience motherhood with more lightness and truth.</p>
<p>📌 And remember: seeking support is not selfishness. It is wisdom. You deserve to be well in order to care — including for yourself.</p>
</p>
<p><em>Written by: Lili Fraga &#8211; Family &amp; Children Intercultural &amp; Emotional Development</em></p>
<p><em><strong>Connections that strengthen immigrant wellbeing</strong></em></p>
<p><u><a href="https://www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve/" rel="nofollow">https://www.instagram.com/lilifraga.vivaleve/</a></u></p></p>
',
