		'primavera-chegou-um-convite-para-renovar-tambem-a-sua-alimentacao' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'spring-has-arrived-an-invitation-to-renew-your-nutrition',
			'en_title'            => '🌿 Spring Has Arrived: An Invitation to Renew Your Nutrition Too',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'Spring changes more than the landscape — it is also the perfect moment to bring more colour, freshness and nourishment to your plate.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'A gentle nutritionist\'s guide to eating with spring in Ireland: seasonal food, pulses, fish, olive oil and simple everyday steps.',
			'en_content'          => '<p>Text: Nutri Marcela</p>
<p>Instagram: <u><a href="https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/" rel="nofollow">https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/</a></u></p>
</p>
<p>Spring has arrived, and with it the days grow longer… The <em>daffodils</em> (those beautiful yellow flowers) begin to appear everywhere, and a new energy of renewal, after winter, starts to emerge here in Ireland.</p>
<p>And along with that energy, this is also a great moment to look at our food with more care. To think about how spring can be a great ally for lighter, more nutritious and more balanced eating.</p>
<p>When we talk about healthy eating, following the seasons makes all the difference. Seasonal foods also matter a great deal in this process. So when you find products such as <em>Irish carrots</em> or <em>Irish spinach</em>, choose those options, because they tend to be fresher, tastier and better for our planet too.</p>
<p>And today I want to show you how, with small steps, we can make the most of this season to make our food tastier and more nutritious — not only for you, but for your whole family.</p>
<p> </p>
<h5>💚 A little about me</h5>
<p>I am Marcela Ferreira, a nutritionist. I have lived in Ireland for 14 years and I work with a gentle nutrition approach, grounded in science and free of extremes.</p>
<p>My focus is to help you build a way of eating for life, improve your relationship with food and make more conscious choices — whether your goal is to lose weight, look after your health or improve a test result.</p>
<p>Because, more than numbers, how you feel matters. And caring for your diet is not only about what we do today, but about what we can keep up over the years, creating a healthy and sustainable eating pattern.</p>
<p>And that is why thinking about how to eat well in each season is also part of this process.</p>
<p>For example, in winter… who really feels like eating salad? Our body naturally asks for warmer, more comforting meals.</p>
<p>But that is a subject for another conversation. Today let us focus on spring 🌸 and on how we can make our food lighter, more nutritious and more in tune with this new season.</p>
<p> </p>
<p><strong> </strong></p>
<h5>🌸 How to adjust your eating for spring in practice</h5>
<p>Spring here in Ireland still arrives with milder temperatures. It is only around May that we begin to feel a little warmth — or rather, to put away the heavier coats.</p>
<p>We also do not yet have a great variety of local fruit and vegetables at this stage, and that is absolutely fine.</p>
<p>The most important thing here is not perfection, but a simple principle: the more colours on your plate, the better.</p>
<p>If you do not usually include vegetables in your meals yet, start little by little. Try adding at least one vegetable to lunch and dinner.</p>
<p>And an important point: frozen vegetables are excellent allies too. They are just as nutritious as fresh ones. The same goes for frozen fruit.</p>
<p>If you do not like them much, start by choosing the ones you already enjoy. The idea is to gradually bring more colour and variety into your everyday life.</p>
<p>Remember: progress is better than perfection 💚</p>
</p>
<p> </p>
<h5>🥗 Do not forget the pulses</h5>
<p>Another important point is to include more pulses in your diet.</p>
<p>Our traditional rice and beans, so present in our culture, is an incredible example of nourishing, comforting food, and it can, and should, remain part of your routine.</p>
<p>Here in Ireland, you will find a wide variety of beans in supermarkets such as Tesco and Dunnes, as well as in the Brazilian shops.</p>
<p>An important tip is to soak the beans for at least twelve hours and change the water at least once. When cooking, use fresh water and, if you like, add a few drops of vinegar or some bay leaves.</p>
<p>This process helps improve digestion, reduce gas and even makes the beans more nutritious.</p>
<p>And pay attention to the extras. Processed meats such as bacon and sausage should be eaten more occasionally, and not every day.</p>
<p>You can also vary things with other options such as chickpeas, lentils and even hummus. Even the ready-made one from the supermarket can be a good option, or you can make your own version at home.</p>
<p>A practical snack idea is hummus with carrot. Simple, nutritious, and many children take to it very well.</p>
<p>And yes, tinned pulses can be an option too. Give preference to versions with no added salt or preserved in water. Just remember to rinse them well before eating, as this helps reduce excess sodium and can also lessen the digestive discomfort, such as gas, that some people report.</p>
<p>Variety is essential for our health, and it does not have to be perfect every day. Think across the whole week.</p>
</p>
<p> </p>
<h5>🐟 Include fish in a simple way</h5>
<p>Another point that is well worth it in Ireland is making the most of the easy access to fish, such as salmon.</p>
<p>A simple way to prepare it is to season with salt and olive oil and bake at two hundred degrees for about twenty minutes.</p>
<p>You can serve it with potato, carrot and broccoli. Everything can be prepared practically, even in the same pot, with the potato and carrot in water and the broccoli steamed.</p>
</p>
<p> </p>
<h5>🫒 And do not forget the olive oil</h5>
<p>Olive oil is a great ally for your health and it is very affordable here.</p>
<p>Prefer to buy it in a dark glass bottle and store it in the cupboard, away from the light.</p>
<p> </p>
<h5>💧 Hydration counts too</h5>
<p>And a simple but essential reminder: drink water.</p>
<p>Hydration helps your bowel function, your energy, your mood and your body\'s balance.</p>
<p>Finally, here is an invitation for you to reflect, with this renewing energy of spring. What can you improve in your diet today, even in small steps?</p>
</p>
<p>Remember: progress is always better than perfection.</p>
<p>If you feel you need help to organise your eating, look after your health through nutrition, improve your relationship with food or bring more balance to your daily routine, I am here to help you.</p>
<p>Online and in-person appointments in Dublin and Kilcock</p>
</p>
<p>Instagram: <u><a href="https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/" rel="nofollow">https://www.instagram.com/marcela.holisticnutrition/</a></u></p>
</p>
<p>With love,</p>
<p>Nutri Marcela 💚</p>
<p> </p>',
