		),
		'primavera-o-chamado-silencioso-para-florescer-com-consciencia' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'spring-the-silent-call-to-bloom-with-awareness',
			'en_title'            => 'Spring: The Silent Call to Bloom with Awareness',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'Spring does not ask us to rush into results. It invites us to bloom through awareness, patience and the quiet work of tending what we choose to live.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'Spring as a call to grow with awareness: why blooming asks for presence, patience and maturity — not speed or immediate results.',
			'en_content'          => '<p><em><strong>Text: Juliana Gomes</strong></em></p>
<p><em><strong>Founder of the Escola Mulher Sistêmica</strong></em></p>
<p><em><strong>Instagram: </strong></em><u><strong><a href="https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/" rel="nofollow">https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/</a></strong></u></p>
</p>
<p>The arrival of spring in the northern hemisphere is not just a change of season. It is a subtle yet profound invitation to move through a state of transition — one of those that are not always visible on the outside, but that stir our inner world intensely.</p>
</p>
<p>After the withdrawal of winter, life begins to rise again. The light returns, the days expand, nature awakens. And, along with it, something in us moves too. Spring calls us to bloom, to begin new cycles, to open what was previously gestating.</p>
</p>
<p>But blooming is not just about beginning.</p>
<p>Blooming requires presence, support and maturity.</p>
<p>There is a time between the initial impulse and the concrete manifestation of what we wish to live. A time we often neglect, because we were taught to seek quick results, immediate answers and paths without a crossing.</p>
</p>
<p>Spring teaches us the opposite.</p>
<p>It reminds us that everything that is born needs care. That what begins needs to be nourished. That the beauty of blooming lies not only in the result, but in the silent process of growth that happens day after day.</p>
</p>
<p>For us women, this understanding is even more essential. Because life invites us not only to do, but to gestate. To respect our rhythms, our inner timing, our capacity to sustain what we choose to live.</p>
<p>And perhaps the greatest challenge of this time is precisely this: not to lose yourself in the many possibilities that arise, but to remain connected to what truly matters.</p>
</p>
<p>Spring is fertile.</p>
<p>But not everything needs to bloom.</p>
<p>Discerning, choosing and sustaining are movements of feminine maturity.</p>
<p>Returning to awareness of the cycles is returning to our place.</p>
</p>
<p>It is learning to walk with more truth, less haste and more presence.</p>
<p>Spring has arrived.</p>
<p>And with it, the opportunity to bloom — not by impulse, but by awareness.</p>
</p>
<p>At the <em>Escola Mulher Sistêmica</em>, we look at life from this perspective: that we are part of a greater cycle, and that when we disconnect from it, we also lose our own direction.</p>
</p>
<p>Our School is here, close to you, in County Laois, as a living space of reconnection, maturity and belonging to the cycle of life.</p>',
			'link_map'            => array(),
		),
		'proposito-o-sentido-que-nos-move-por-dentro' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'purpose-the-meaning-that-moves-us-from-within',
			'en_title'            => 'Purpose: The Meaning That Moves Us From Within',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'Purpose is not a prize waiting to be found — it is an inner compass we hear with the heart and allow to come alive through what we give.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'A reflection on purpose as an inner compass: why meaning is felt rather than found, and what reconnecting with it asks of us.',
			'en_content'          => '</p>
<p><em><strong>Text: Juliana Gomes</strong></em></p>
<p><em>Founder of the Escola Mulher Sistêmica</em></p>
<p><em>Instagram: </em><u><a href="https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/" rel="nofollow">https://www.instagram.com/juliana.mendes.gomes/</a></u></p>
</p>
</p>
</p>
<p>I received with joy the invitation from dear Tati to write for this magazine for Brazilian women and men in <em>Portlaoise</em>. It is an honour to be able to share reflections on a theme as essential as it is delicate: the purpose of life.</p>
<p>My name is Juliana, I am Brazilian, I am 43 years old and I have lived in Ireland for nine years. I am the mother of Lia, the wife of Thiago and the founder of the <strong>Escola Mulher Sistêmica</strong>, a school of feminine consciousness based on the Natural Laws of Cooperation of Life, laws that reconnect us to the natural cycle of existence, to ancestry and to the deep sense of belonging.</p>
<p>And it is precisely about meaning that I want to talk with you today.</p>
<p>The word “purpose” is one of the most repeated of our era. Many of us look for quick answers: <em>What is my purpose? How do I find it?</em></p>
<p>But perhaps the truest question is another:</p>
<p><strong>What if purpose is not something you find, but something that reveals itself when there is space?</strong></p>
<p>Purpose is not just a mental concept. It is an intimate, spiritual and emotional perception. We women especially, so burdened by external demands, often try to understand life only with our heads, when purpose can only be heard with the heart.</p>
<p>In my experience, purpose is not to be confused with who we are, nor is it limited to what we do professionally. Purpose is that which moves us from within. It is a sense of direction, like an inner compass pointing to where the soul wishes to walk.</p>
<p>When we are aligned with that sense, life gains more harmony. But, in the crossings of being human, we often lose our way. And this happens because we live disconnected from our essence and from the natural cycle of life.</p>
<p>I like to think of purpose as we think of our children: we conceive them, care for them, educate them, but they do not belong to us. One day, they take on the world. Purpose is like that too. It does not stay tied to us. It needs to come alive. It is an offering.</p>
<p>A great part of the emotional illness of our era is born precisely from this disconnection: burnout, anxiety, chronic stress. It is not only an excess of tasks, it is the absence of meaning in what you give.</p>
<p>Reconnection, however, can begin simply. A few minutes of silence. A conscious breath. A prayer. Intimate writing. A sincere request to return to your essence.</p>
<p>Because it is not only <em>what we do</em>, but <em>how we do it</em>.</p>
<p>Sometimes, it will be necessary to recalculate the route. In some cases, purpose lies within the current path, but with a truer “how”. In others, it will be necessary to listen to an old call that has long been waiting for courage.</p>
<p>And it is important to remember: purpose is not a prize for those who search, but a process for those who surrender.</p>
<p>May this text be just a small opening. An invitation for you to breathe and ask yourself:</p>
<p><strong>What in me wants to come alive now?</strong></p>
<p><strong>Which meaning wants to move me?</strong></p>
</p>
<p>Gratitude</p>
</p>
<p><strong>Juliana</strong></p>
<p>Founder of the Escola Mulher Sistêmica</p>
<p> </p>
<p> </p></p>',
			'link_map'            => array(),
		),
		'quando-a-terra-desperta-a-primavera-e-o-novo-ano-astrologico-1' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'when-the-earth-awakens-spring-and-the-new-astrological-year',
			'en_title'            => 'When the Earth Awakens: Spring and the New Astrological Year',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'Spring arrives as a quiet turn of energy, and with the Sun in Aries it opens a new astrological year of movement, direction and gentle courage.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'Why spring and the astrological new year ask for gentle courage, conscious movement and a living intention the body can feel.',
			'en_content'          => '<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p>
<p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p>
</p>
<p>There is a moment in the year when energy changes direction — quietly, almost</p>
<p>imperceptibly. It is like when the body wakes before the alarm clock and something inside you</p>
<p>starts to flow again.</p>
<p>Spring begins like this.</p>
<p>After the withdrawal of winter, life pushes outwards again. The light increases, the air</p>
<p>changes, and a natural desire arises to reorganise, move the body and decide what was previously</p>
<p>on hold.</p>
</p>
<p>Astrologically, this portal coincides with the entry of the <em><strong>Sun into Aries — the beginning of the</strong></em></p>
<p><em><strong>New Astrological Year.</strong></em></p>
<p>If January opens the social calendar, March opens the calendar of the soul.</p>
<p>Aries represents the first impulse of life — the seed that breaks through the earth. It does not ask for</p>
<p>perfection, it asks for movement. That is why, at this stage, many people feel more energy,</p>
<p>positive restlessness and the need to begin something new.</p>
</p>
<p>After the dive of Pisces, which cleaned and closed cycles, the question arises:</p>
<p>“And now, what are you going to do with who you have become?”</p>
<p>Spring is not only about flowers — it is about direction.</p>
</p>
<p>In mental health, this is an important period for choosing where to place your energy. The</p>
<p>mind wants to act, but alignment comes from awareness, not from excess. Ask yourself: what</p>
<p>really deserves my attention now?</p>
</p>
<p>Emotionally, the season asks for a gentle courage — allowing yourself to take up more space and</p>
<p>to seek relationships more coherent with who you are today.</p>
<p>In the physical body, the energy of Aries needs movement so that it does not turn into tension. Walking</p>
<p>more, stretching, returning to physical activity and receiving natural light help the nervous system to leave</p>
<p>winter mode.</p>
</p>
<p>In practical life, this is the ideal moment to start projects, reorganise routines and test</p>
<p>new directions without demanding immediate results. It is a phase of conscious</p>
<p>experimentation.</p>
</p>
<p>Aligning yourself with the New Astrological Year is not about changing everything at once, but acting from</p>
<p>a truer place.</p>
<p>If winter asked “what needs to end?” spring asks “what deserves</p>
<p>to be born?”</p>
</p>
<p>Choosing a living intention — something that can be felt in the body — is already enough to</p>
<p>open the way.</p>
<p>The New Astrological Year is an organic new beginning. A whisper from nature saying:</p>
<p>“Now you can move forward.”</p>
</p>
<p>The invitation is to move, without losing the inner listening that winter taught — so that action</p>
<p>and awareness walk together, and this new cycle is lived with presence, direction and</p>
<p>vitality.</p>
</p>
<p>Aria Milioni</p>
</p>
<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p>
<p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p></p>',
			'link_map'            => array(),
		),
		'quem-voce-se-tornou-longe-de-casa' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'who-have-you-become-away-from-home',
			'en_title'            => 'Who Have You Become Away From Home?',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'A silent trap hides inside the immigration experience — and it has nothing to do with the airport.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'A systemic therapist on what emigration does to your emotional support network, and how to rebuild it in Ireland.',
			'en_content'          => '<p><u><em><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></em></u></p>
<p><u><em><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></em></u></p>
</p>
<p>Instagram: <u><a href="https://www.instagram.com/ariaenergyhealing/" rel="nofollow">https://www.instagram.com/ariaenergyhealing/</a></u></p>
</p>
<p>There is a silent trap inside the immigration experience.</p>
<p>It does not happen at the airport.</p>
<p>It does not happen when you say goodbye to your family.</p>
<p>Nor when you realise you have missed birthdays, weddings or important moments of</p>
<p>those who stayed behind.</p>
<p>It happens little by little.</p>
<p>So slowly that it often goes unnoticed.</p>
<p>Because emigrating does not mean only changing country.</p>
</p>
<p>It means inevitably losing a part of the system that regulated your emotional</p>
<p>life. And that is something few immigrants understand when they arrive.</p>
<p>When we lived near family, friends or the people who knew us</p>
<p>deeply, there was an invisible network holding up our emotional balance.</p>
<p>A spontaneous conversation. A Sunday lunch. An unexpected visit. A hug</p>
<p>with no need to put it in the calendar.</p>
<p>All of this helped our nervous system remember that we were safe.</p>
<p>When we emigrate, that network disappears.</p>
</p>
<p>And however many new friendships we build, there is a truth few people like to</p>
<p>admit:</p>
<p>Part of the isolation is inevitable. It does not matter how sociable you are. It does not matter</p>
<p>how many friends you make. It does not matter how much you love the country where you live.</p>
<p>There is a period, lasting months for some people and years for others, in which the</p>
<p>feeling of not fully belonging anywhere is part of the journey.</p>
<p>You no longer live the life you left behind. But you do not yet feel</p>
<p>completely part of the new reality either.</p>
<p>It is an emotional no man\'s land.</p>
<p>And it is exactly in this space that some of the greatest vulnerabilities</p>
<p>of the immigrant begin to appear.</p>
<p>The first of them is projection.</p>
<p>Without realising it, we start to expect new relationships to fill spaces that were once</p>
<p>occupied by an entire community.</p>
</p>
<p>We expect a partner to be best friend, therapist, family and safe harbour all at the same</p>
<p>time.</p>
<p>We expect recent friendships to have the depth of decades.</p>
<p>We expect people we met a few months ago to understand pain built up</p>
<p>over a whole lifetime.</p>
<p>And when that does not happen, the feeling of abandonment appears.</p>
<p>But often we are not suffering because of the relationship itself. We are suffering because of the</p>
<p>absence of the network that existed before it.</p>
<p>The problem is that there is a second factor that makes all of this even more intense. And it</p>
<p>rarely receives the attention it deserves.</p>
<p>The body. Especially when we talk about physical activity.</p>
<p>Many immigrants, especially those who came from sunny countries, go through</p>
<p>a drastic change of lifestyle.</p>
<p>Less time outdoors. Less exposure to the sun. Less movement. Fewer spontaneous</p>
<p>walks. Fewer in-person meet-ups. Less human contact.</p>
<p>And without realising it, they enter a dangerous cycle.</p>
</p>
<h5>They feel more isolated.</h5>
<p>They go out less. They move less.</p>
<p>And the less they move, the worse they feel.</p>
<p>What few people know is that movement is not only for losing weight or</p>
<p>improving physical health.</p>
</p>
<h5>Movement is a tool for emotional regulation.</h5>
<p>When we walk, run, train or simply put the body in</p>
<p>motion, we help the nervous system to process stress, anxiety, frustration and</p>
<p>sadness.</p>
<p>Physical activity acts as a kind of biological release valve.</p>
<p>It reduces stress levels. It improves mood. It promotes sleep. It increases the feeling of</p>
<p>energy.</p>
<p>And, perhaps most importantly for the immigrant, it creates an inner sense of stability.</p>
<p>Because there is something nobody tells us about immigration: not all longing is</p>
<p>emotional.</p>
</p>
<p>Sometimes what we are interpreting as missing home is exhaustion. Sometimes what looks like</p>
<p>deep sadness is a lack of sunlight. Sometimes what looks like an existential crisis is a</p>
<p>nervous system that has gone months without discharging accumulated tension.</p>
<p>And no, this does not mean that the pain of distance is not real.</p>
<p>It is.</p>
<p>The longing exists. The losses exist. The missed birthdays exist. The absences</p>
<p>exist.</p>
<p>But when the body is exhausted, sedentary and deprived of natural light, everything becomes bigger.</p>
<p>Rejection feels more painful.</p>
<p>Loneliness feels deeper.</p>
<p>Anxiety feels more intense.</p>
<p>Conflicts seem more serious.</p>
<p>Goodbyes seem more final.</p>
<p>That is why one of the biggest mistakes an immigrant can make is to believe that all of their</p>
<p>emotional challenges will be solved through the mind alone.</p>
<p>Analysis is essential, but it does not feed the needs of the physical body.</p>
<p>In the vast majority of cases, the only real need is more movement.</p>
<p>More walking. More nature. More fresh air. More contact with the physical world.</p>
<p>Especially during the long winter months.</p>
<p>Because winter does not affect only mood. It affects our perception of life.</p>
<p>For someone who grew up surrounded by sun, consecutive months of grey sky can</p>
<p>reduce energy, motivation and the emotional capacity to deal with challenges.</p>
<p>And when we add that to the inevitable isolation of immigration, the effects become even</p>
<p>more evident.</p>
<p>That is why creating a routine of movement is not a luxury. It is not vanity. It is not merely</p>
<p>a health matter.</p>
<p>It is a strategy for emotional survival.</p>
<h5>It is a way of telling the nervous system: &#8220;We are far from home, but we are</h5>
<h5>safe.&#8221;</h5>
</p>
<p>Perhaps the greatest lesson of immigration is realising that belonging is not born only from</p>
<p>the people around us.</p>
<p>It is also born from the relationship we build with ourselves.</p>
<p>And that relationship is not created through thoughts alone.</p>
<p>It is created through the small daily choices that help the body, the mind and the</p>
<p>heart to walk together.</p>
<p>So the next time you feel that longing is crushing your chest, ask</p>
<p>a simple question before concluding that something is wrong:</p>
<p>Are you really missing home&#8230;</p>
<p>Or has it been weeks since your body has been begging to get out of the chair, see daylight and</p>
<p>remind your nervous system that there is still life happening out there?</p>
<p>With love,</p>
<p>Aria Milioni</p>
',
			'link_map'            => array(),
		),
		'sol-e-lua-equilibrando-consciencia-e-emocoesdurante-o-inverno-norte-europeu' => array(
			'en_slug'             => 'sun-and-moon-balancing-awareness-and-emotions',
			'en_title'            => 'Sun and Moon: Balancing Awareness and Emotions Through the Northern European Winter',
			'en_excerpt'          => 'The Sun and the Moon shape both our awareness and our emotions — and through the long northern winter, keeping them in balance takes small, steady choices.',
			'en_meta_description' => 'How the Sun and Moon influence awareness and emotion, plus five practical ways to stay balanced through the dark northern European winter.',
			'en_content'          => '<p><u><strong>Text written by: Aria Milioni</strong></u></p>
<p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p>
</p>
</p>
<p> The Sun and the Moon play a fascinating role in our lives, influencing both our awareness and our emotions. </p>
<p>The Sun, with its majestic presence, is our central star, providing the vital energy that keeps us alive. It symbolises awareness, self-affirmation and the ability to express our own light in the world. When we think of the Sun, we can imagine the strength and confidence it inspires in us, like a powerful and just lion. </p>
<p>On the other hand, the Moon is the queen of emotions and of emotional intelligence. It reflects the light of the Sun and is intimately connected to our most sensitive side. The Moon not only influences the tides and nature, but also our feelings, intuitions and memories. Each lunar phase affects us in a unique way, from the introspection of the new moon to the intensity of the full moon. </p>
<p>During the winter in northern Europe, when the days are short and the nights long, keeping mental and emotional balance can be challenging. Here are a few practical tips to help you face this season:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Expose yourself to natural light whenever possible: Make the most of sunny moments to help regulate your biological clock and improve your mood.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Practise physical activity regularly: Exercise not only warms the body, but also releases endorphins, which are essential for mental wellbeing.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Keep a routine: Having fixed times to wake up, eat and sleep helps maintain emotional stability.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Cultivate moments of self-care: Meditation, relaxing reading or a warm bath can help calm the mind and nourish the emotions.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>Connect with other people: Even if the weather invites isolation, keeping in touch with friends and family is crucial for emotional support.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>It is interesting to note that various cultures around the world have different interpretations of the influence of the Sun and the Moon on our lives. While some see the Sun as a symbol of authority and leadership, others may associate it with a spiritual guide. In the same way, the Moon, in its constant change, is seen as a magical and seductive force that invites us to reflect and to connect with our inner self.</p>
<p>Would you like to know more about how these celestial forces can influence your day-to-day life? Leave your comment and I will bring more information in the next article</p>
</p>
<p><u><strong>Aria Milioni</strong></u></p>
<p><u><strong>Systemic Therapist</strong></u></p></p>',
			'link_map'            => array(),
		),
